# Site signals

Leave `compliance.mdc`, `content.mdc`, and `voice.mdc` in charge of YMYL, section shape,
and tone.

## Answer near the top

On guides, FAQs, comparisons, and landings, answer the page's primary query in the
first one or two paragraphs (or the first sentence after the H1). Then expand.

Keep body paragraphs short: usually one or two sentences, three at most when a
claim needs an inline citation beside it. Split dense blocks instead of stacking
facts in one wall of text (`voice.mdc`).

Do not open with brand story, "in this article," or a history lesson. Boolean and
short-fact pages still follow `content.mdc` (Yes/No or the fact first). Skip this
on product specs, checkout, and legal pages.

Each informational H2/H3 still leads with its own one-sentence answer (`content.mdc`).
The page-level answer and the section-level answers are both required.

## Title, H1, and early wording

Follow `three-kings.mdc`: the primary keyword (the phrase the URL is built for) must appear in the title tag, the single H1, and the first body sentence.

One H1. It should name the same topic as the title and the URL, in natural language.
Do not stuff the keyword into every H2.

## Site focus

Keep new URLs inside the catalog we sell. If a page's job already exists, update
that URL and link to it. Do not publish a thin "supporting" article to occupy a
keyword variant.

## Originality

Do not rewrite MedlinePlus, DailyMed, or a competitor PDP. Add information gain
from supplied sources only (live prices, variants, shipping, payment, comparisons
we can defend). If that material is missing, mark `[NEEDS: …]`.

There is no target word count. Stop when the query is satisfied.

## Authors, reviews, and dates

Bylines and "Reviewed By" only for a real person who reviewed the piece.
Do not invent reviewers, credentials, or customer quotes. Reviews and UGC must be
real.

`datePublished` and `dateModified` (and matching JSON-LD) must be true. Bump
modified only when visible content changed. Do not fake freshness on stable
medical facts.

## Clicks, links, and media

Write the title and first screen so they match the query.

Internal links: descriptive anchors, from a relevant page, because the reader
needs the destination. Do not dump exact-match anchors in the footer or every
paragraph.

Images must belong on the page. Descriptive alt; no keyword-stuffed filenames
or decorative stock used as "content."

Use one consistent name for each product or drug on the page (then a short alias
if needed). URLs stay short and readable — not `/buy-modafinil-modafinil-online/`.
